% ABSTRACT
% --------------------------------------------------

\begin{abstract}

In this paper, we study a nonlinear mathematical model and investigate
its analytical and numerical properties. We derive the governing
equations, discuss the existence of solutions, and examine the behavior
of the model under different parameter values. Numerical results are
presented to illustrate the theoretical findings.

\textbf{Keywords:}
nonlinear equations, mathematical modeling, numerical analysis,
differential equations

\end{abstract}

% --------------------------------------------------
% 1. INTRODUCTION
% --------------------------------------------------

\section{Introduction}

Mathematical models are widely used to describe physical, biological,
engineering, and economic systems. Differential equations provide a
powerful framework for describing the evolution of such systems.

Consider the general nonlinear differential equation

\begin{equation}
\frac{dx}{dt} = f(x,t),
\label{eq:general}
\end{equation}

where $x(t)$ represents the state variable and $f(x,t)$ is a nonlinear
function.

The main objective of this paper is to investigate the mathematical
properties of Equation~\eqref{eq:general} and demonstrate how analytical
and numerical methods can be used to study its solutions.

The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section~2
introduces the mathematical model. Section~3 presents the analytical
analysis. Section~4 discusses the numerical method. Section~5 presents
the results, and Section~6 concludes the paper.

% --------------------------------------------------
% 2. MATHEMATICAL MODEL
% --------------------------------------------------

\section{Mathematical Model}

We consider the nonlinear model

\begin{equation}
\frac{dx}{dt} = ax(1-x),
\label{eq:logistic}
\end{equation}

where $a>0$ is a model parameter.

The initial condition is given by

\begin{equation}
x(0)=x_0,
\label{eq:initial}
\end{equation}

where $0<x_0<1$.

Equation~\eqref{eq:logistic} is a classical nonlinear model. It
describes a quantity that initially grows approximately exponentially
but eventually approaches a limiting value.

% --------------------------------------------------
% 3. ANALYTICAL SOLUTION
% --------------------------------------------------

\section{Analytical Analysis}

Equation~\eqref{eq:logistic} can be solved using separation of
variables.

We write

\[
\frac{dx}{x(1-x)} = a\,dt.
\]

Using partial fractions,

\[
\frac{1}{x(1-x)}
=
\frac{1}{x}
+
\frac{1}{1-x}.
\]

Therefore,

\[
\int
\left(
\frac{1}{x}
+
\frac{1}{1-x}
\right)dx
=
\int a\,dt.
\]

After integration, we obtain

\[
\ln|x|-\ln|1-x|=at+C.
\]

Thus,

\[
\ln\left(\frac{x}{1-x}\right)=at+C.
\]

Exponentiating both sides gives

\[
\frac{x}{1-x}=Ce^{at}.
\]

Solving for $x$, we obtain

\begin{equation}
x(t)
=
\frac{Ce^{at}}{1+Ce^{at}}.
\label{eq:solution_general}
\end{equation}

Using the initial condition $x(0)=x_0$, we find

\[
C=\frac{x_0}{1-x_0}.
\]

Hence the solution is

\begin{equation}
x(t)
=
\frac{x_0e^{at}}
{1-x_0+x_0e^{at}}.
\label{eq:solution}
\end{equation}

Equation~\eqref{eq:solution} is the exact analytical solution of the
model.

% --------------------------------------------------
% 4. EQUILIBRIUM ANALYSIS
% --------------------------------------------------

\section{Equilibrium Analysis}

The equilibrium points are obtained by setting

\[
\frac{dx}{dt}=0.
\]

Therefore,

\[
ax(1-x)=0.
\]

Assuming $a\neq0$, the equilibrium solutions are

\[
x_1^*=0,
\qquad
x_2^*=1.
\]

To investigate their stability, we define

\[
f(x)=ax(1-x).
\]

Then

\[
f'(x)=a(1-2x).
\]

At $x=0$,

\[
f'(0)=a>0,
\]

so $x=0$ is an unstable equilibrium.

At $x=1$,

\[
f'(1)=-a<0,
\]

so $x=1$ is a stable equilibrium.

Thus, for $a>0$ and $0<x_0<1$,

\[
\lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}x(t)=1.
\]

% --------------------------------------------------
% 5. NUMERICAL METHOD
% --------------------------------------------------

\section{Numerical Method}

Although the analytical solution is available for this particular
model, numerical methods are useful for more complicated nonlinear
systems.

We use the forward Euler method. For a differential equation

\[
\frac{dx}{dt}=f(x,t),
\]

the Euler approximation is

\begin{equation}
x_{n+1}
=
x_n+h f(x_n,t_n),
\label{eq:euler}
\end{equation}

where $h$ is the time step.

For Equation~\eqref{eq:logistic}, this becomes

\begin{equation}
x_{n+1}
=
x_n
+
ha x_n(1-x_n).
\label{eq:euler_logistic}
\end{equation}

The numerical solution can then be compared with the exact solution
given by Equation~\eqref{eq:solution}.

