% --------------------------------------------------
% 8. DISCUSSION
% --------------------------------------------------

\section{Discussion}

The mathematical analysis shows that the model possesses two
equilibrium points. The equilibrium $x=0$ is unstable, whereas $x=1$
is stable for $a>0$.

The analytical solution demonstrates that the system approaches the
stable equilibrium as time increases. The numerical Euler method also
provides an approximation of the solution, although its accuracy
depends on the selected time step $h$.

For more complicated nonlinear systems, an exact solution may not be
available. In such cases, numerical methods such as the Runge--Kutta
method, finite-difference methods, or spectral methods may be required.

% --------------------------------------------------
% 9. CONCLUSION
% --------------------------------------------------

\section{Conclusion}

In this paper, we investigated a nonlinear mathematical model using
analytical and numerical techniques. An exact solution was obtained
using separation of variables, and the stability of the equilibrium
points was analyzed.

The results demonstrate that the solution approaches the stable
equilibrium as time tends to infinity. Numerical methods provide a
useful alternative when analytical solutions are difficult or
impossible to obtain.

Future work may consider extensions of the model involving multiple
variables, time-dependent parameters, stochastic effects, or spatial
diffusion.

